This can obviously be done in a shader, but this requires to write or find the right code every time and is cumbersome to do repeatedly even within the same project.
A simple shader code example of conversion from a height value to a 2D normal vector is this:

```GLSL
vec2 stdNormalMap(float height) 
{
    return -vec2(dFdx(height), dFdy(height)) * bump_strength + .5;
}
```
In this case, the generic `height` parameter can either come from a texture or be procedurally generated. A Sobel filter would be an even better "converter".
